question:
What is the output of the following Java code?

int x = 5;
int y = x++ + ++x;
System.out.println(y);

In the following Java snippet, what will be printed?

String s = "Hello";
s.concat(" World");
System.out.println(s);

Identify the bug in this Java code that is supposed to swap two integers without a temporary variable:

a = a + b;
b = a - b;
a = a - b;

What will this Java code print, and why?

Integer a = 127;
Integer b = 127;
System.out.println(a == b);

What is the output of the following code involving Java's integer division?

System.out.println(7 / 2);
System.out.println(7.0 / 2);

Given this Java code snippet using a StringBuilder, predict the final value of sb:

StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("abc");
sb.reverse();
sb.append("xyz");

What gets printed by the following Java code that catches a division-by-zero exception?

try {
    int x = 5 / 0;
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Error");
} finally {
    System.out.println("Done");
}

Predict the output:

int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};
for (int i : arr) {
    i = i * 2;
}
System.out.println(arr[0]);

What is the output of this switch statement with fall-through?

int day = 2;
switch(day) {
    case 1: System.out.print("A");
    case 2: System.out.print("B");
    case 3: System.out.print("C");
        break;
    default: System.out.print("D");
}

Identify why the following Java code fails to compile:

public void printValue() {
    int x;
    System.out.println(x);
}

What is the output of the following code involving the string pool and the new keyword?

String a = "test";
String b = new String("test");
System.out.println(a == b);

Predict the output of this nested loop with a labeled break:

outer:
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        if (j == 1) break outer;
        System.out.print("" + i + j);
    }
}

What will be printed by the following code that causes integer overflow?

int max = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
System.out.println(max + 1);

Identify the bug in this Python code meant to compute the sum of squares from 1 to n:

def sum_of_squares(n):
    total = 0
    for i in range(n):
        total += i * i
    return total

Debug this Python code intended to remove all vowels from a string:

def remove_vowels(s):
    for ch in s:
        if ch in "aeiou":
            s = s.replace(ch, "")
    return s

Predict the output of the following code involving exception handling with else and finally:

try:
    x = 10 / 2
except ZeroDivisionError:
    print("Error")
else:
    print("No error")
finally:
    print("Done")

What is the output of the following C code?

int x = 5;
printf("%d %d %d", x, x++, ++x);

Identify the bug in this C code meant to reverse an array in place:

void reverse(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[n-i];
        arr[n-i] = temp;
    }
}

What will the following pointer arithmetic code print?

int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};
int *p = arr;
printf("%d", *(p + 2));

Predict the output of the following code involving struct alignment and sizeof:

struct Point { int x; char c; };
printf("%lu", sizeof(struct Point));

What is the output of this code, which passes an array to a function and prints its sizeof inside that function?

void printSize(int arr[]) {
    printf("%lu", sizeof(arr));
}
int main() {
    int arr[10];
    printSize(arr);
}

Debug the following C code that is supposed to allocate memory for a 5-element integer array dynamically:

int *arr = malloc(5);
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) arr[i] = i;

What will this C code involving a static local variable print across multiple calls?

void counter() {
    static int count = 0;
    count++;
    printf("%d ", count);
}
int main() {
    counter(); counter(); counter();
}

Predict the output of the following code with the comma operator:

int x = (1, 2, 3);
printf("%d", x);

What is wrong with this C code that tries to return a pointer to a local variable?

int* getValue() {
    int x = 10;
    return &x;
}

What will the following macro expansion code output?

#define SQUARE(x) x*x
printf("%d", SQUARE(2+3));

Predict the output of this code involving character arithmetic:

char c = 'A';
printf("%c", c + 2);

Debug the following code meant to copy one string into another using a custom function:

void mystrcpy(char *dest, char *src) {
    while (*src) {
        dest = src;
        src++;
    }
}

What is the output of the following code involving operator precedence with the ternary operator?

int a = 5, b = 2, c;
c = a > b ? a++ : ++b;
printf("%d %d %d", a, b, c);

Predict the output of this code with a do-while loop and a break condition:

int i = 0;
do {
    if (i == 3) break;
    printf("%d", i);
    i++;
} while (i < 5);

What is the output of the following C++ code involving references?

int x = 10;
int &y = x;
y = 20;
cout << x;

Predict the output of this C++ code involving default arguments:

void show(int a, int b = 10) { cout << a + b; }
int main() { show(5); }

What is wrong with this C++ code that attempts to delete a dynamically allocated array?

int* arr = new int[10];
delete arr;

Predict the output of the following code involving copy constructors:

class A {
public:
    A() { cout << "default "; }
    A(const A &a) { cout << "copy "; }
};
A a1;
A a2 = a1;

What will this code involving const correctness output or fail to compile?

void display(const int &x) {
    x++;
    cout << x;
}

Predict the output of the following code involving pointers to pointers:

int x = 5;
int *p = &x;
int **pp = &p;
cout << **pp;

What is the output of the following code with a static class member shared across instances?

class Counter {
public:
    static int count;
    Counter() { count++; }
};
int Counter::count = 0;
Counter c1, c2, c3;
cout << Counter::count;

Predict the output of the following code involving multiple catch blocks:

try {
    throw 3.14;
} catch (int e) {
    cout << "int";
} catch (double e) {
    cout << "double";
}

What will the following code involving an array of objects print?

class Point {
public:
    int x = 1;
};
Point arr[3];
cout << arr[1].x;

Predict the output of the following code involving bitwise operator precedence:

int a = 5, b = 3;
cout << (a & b) << " " << (a | b);

What is the output of this code involving multiple inheritance and explicit scope resolution?

class A { public: void show() { cout << "A"; } };
class B { public: void show() { cout << "B"; } };
class C : public A, public B {};
C obj;
obj.A::show();

Predict the output of the following code involving a function returning a reference:

int& getRef(int arr[], int i) { return arr[i]; }
int main() { int arr[3] = {1,2,3}; getRef(arr, 1) = 99; cout << arr[1]; }

What will this code involving the this pointer and method chaining output?

class Box {
    int side;
public:
    Box& setSide(int s) { side = s; return *this; }
    int getSide() { return side; }
};
Box b;
cout << b.setSide(5).getSide();

What is the output of the following JavaScript code involving variable hoisting?

console.log(x);
var x = 5;

Predict the output of this code involving closures:

function outer() {
    let count = 0;
    return function() {
        count++;
        return count;
    };
}
const counter = outer();
console.log(counter());
console.log(counter());

What will the following code involving == versus === print?

console.log(0 == "0");
console.log(0 === "0");
console.log(null == undefined);

Predict the output of this code involving the event loop and setTimeout:

console.log("A");
setTimeout(() => console.log("B"), 0);
console.log("C");

What is the output of the following code involving array destructuring with rest syntax?

const [a, b, ...rest] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
console.log(a, b, rest);

Predict the output of this code involving 'this' inside an arrow function versus a regular function:

const obj = {
    value: 42,
    regular: function() { return this.value; },
    arrow: () => this.value
};
console.log(obj.regular());
console.log(obj.arrow());

What will this code involving chained array methods print?

const arr = [1,2,3,4,5];
console.log(arr.filter(n => n % 2 === 0).map(n => n * n).reduce((a,b) => a+b));

Predict the output of the following code involving type coercion in string concatenation:

console.log(1 + "2" + 3);
console.log(1 + 2 + "3");

What is the output of this code involving var inside a loop combined with setTimeout?

for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}

Predict the output of the following code involving object property shorthand with computed keys:

const key = "name";
const obj = { [key]: "Alice" };
console.log(obj.name);

What will this code involving the default numeric sort behavior of Array.sort() print?

const arr = [10, 1, 21, 2];
console.log(arr.sort());

Predict the output of the following code involving optional chaining and the nullish coalescing operator:

const user = { profile: null };
console.log(user.profile?.name ?? "No name");

What is the output of this code involving an uncaught rejection inside an async function?

async function fetchData() {
    throw new Error("Failed");
}
fetchData().catch(e => console.log(e.message));

Predict the output of this Java code involving explicit narrowing type casting:

double d = 9.99;
int i = (int) d;
System.out.println(i);

What will the following JavaScript code print due to dynamic typing and coercion?

let x = "5";
x = x - 2;
console.log(x, typeof x);

What is the output of the following C++ code involving auto type deduction for an integer and a floating-point literal?

auto x = 5;
auto y = 5.5;
cout << typeid(x).name() << " " << typeid(y).name();

Predict the output of this Java code involving implicit char-to-int conversion:

char c = 'a';
int x = c + 1;
System.out.println(x);

What is the output of this JavaScript code involving comparisons with NaN?

console.log(NaN === NaN);
console.log(Number.isNaN(NaN));

What will the following C code print due to implicit type promotion in mixed arithmetic?

int a = 5;
float b = 2.0;
printf("%f", a / b);

What is the output of this Java code involving the Integer wrapper class's cache for small values?

Integer a = 200;
Integer b = 200;
System.out.println(a == b);

What is the output of the following Java code involving a chain of nested ternary operators?

int x = 5;
String r = (x > 10) ? "big" : (x > 3) ? "medium" : "small";
System.out.println(r);

Predict the output of the following C code involving compound assignment operators evaluated in sequence:

int a = 5;
a += a -= a * a;
printf("%d", a);

Predict the output of the following JavaScript code involving division by zero:

console.log(typeof (1/0));
console.log(1/0);

What is the output of the following C code, given a = 6 and b = 3, involving a dangling else?

if (a > 5)
    if (b > 5)
        printf("Both");
else
    printf("Not both");

What is the output of the following JavaScript switch statement, which omits break statements for two consecutive matching cases?

let val = 1;
switch(val) {
    case 1:
    case 2:
        console.log("one or two");
        break;
    default:
        console.log("other");
}

Predict the output of the following C++ code, where a short-circuiting condition prevents a division by zero:

int a = 0;
if (a != 0 && 10 / a > 1) cout << "yes"; else cout << "no";

Predict the output of the following Python code involving an elif chain where two conditions could both be true:

x = 15
if x > 20:
    print("large")
elif x > 10:
    print("medium")
elif x > 5:
    print("small")
else:
    print("tiny")

What is the output of the following C code, where an assignment is mistakenly used instead of a comparison inside an if condition?

int x = 5;
if (x = 0) {
    printf("zero");
} else {
    printf("not zero");
}

Predict the output of the following Java for loop and identify why it does not print the expected five values:

for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.print(i * i + " ");
}

What is the output of the following Python loop involving the continue statement?

for i in range(5):
    if i == 3:
        continue
    print(i)

Predict the output of the following C loop involving the continue statement:

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 2) continue;
    printf("%d", i);
}

What is the output of the following JavaScript while loop that increments by two each iteration?

let i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    console.log(i);
    i += 2;
}

Predict the output of the following C++ do-while loop, and explain why its body executes even though the condition is initially false:

int i = 10;
do {
    cout << i;
} while (i < 5);

Predict the output of the following Python nested loop involving a break inside the inner loop only:

for i in range(3):
    for j in range(3):
        if j == i:
            break
        print(i, j)

What is the output of the following C code involving the post-decrement operator directly inside a while condition?

int i = 3;
while (i--) {
    printf("%d ", i);
}

Predict the output of the following JavaScript nested for loop that skips iterations where the indices are equal:

for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        if (i === j) continue;
        console.log(i, j);
    }
}

Predict the output of the following C for loop that uses multiple initialization and update expressions separated by commas:

for (int i = 0, j = 5; i < j; i++, j--) {
    printf("%d-%d ", i, j);
}

What is the output of the following JavaScript Immediately Invoked Function Expression (IIFE)?

(function() {
    console.log("executed");
})();

What undefined behavior does the following C code risk by accessing one index past the end of the array?

int arr[5] = {1,2,3,4,5};
printf("%d", arr[5]);

The following Java code is supposed to find the maximum element in an array but returns an incorrect result whenever all elements are negative. Identify and fix the bug.

int max = 0;
for (int num : arr) {
    if (num > max) max = num;
}

Debug the following Python code, which is meant to check whether a number is prime but incorrectly classifies 1 as prime.

def is_prime(n):
    for i in range(2, n):
        if n % i == 0:
            return False
    return True

The following C code is meant to compute the sum of an array but crashes with a segmentation fault. Identify the cause.

int sum(int *arr, int n) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) total += arr[i];
    return total;
}

Debug the following JavaScript code, which is supposed to filter even numbers from an array but always returns an empty array.

function evens(arr) {
    return arr.filter(n => n % 2 = 0);
}

The following C++ code is meant to implement binary search but enters an infinite loop for certain inputs. Identify the bug.

int low = 0, high = n - 1;
while (low <= high) {
    int mid = (low + high) / 2;
    if (arr[mid] < target) low = mid;
    else high = mid;
}

Debug the following Java code, where a for-each loop is meant to double every element of a list, but the original list is left unchanged.

for (int num : list) {
    num = num * 2;
}

Debug the following C code, where a recursive function meant to compute a factorial produces an incorrect result for an input of 0.

int factorial(int n) {
    if (n == 1) return 1;
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

The following JavaScript code implements a countdown timer using setInterval but never stops counting. Identify the bug.

let count = 5;
setInterval(() => {
    console.log(count);
    count--;
}, 1000);

The following C++ code is supposed to copy a vector, but modifying the copy also unexpectedly changes the original. Identify the bug.

vector<int>& copyVec(vector<int>& original) {
    return original;
}

Debug the following Java code, where a string comparison using == unexpectedly fails for two strings that appear identical.

String a = new String("test");
String b = "test";
if (a == b) {
    System.out.println("equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("not equal");
}

The following JavaScript code implements a counter using a closure, but calling the returned function always resets the count to zero. Identify the bug.

function makeCounter() {
    let count = 0;
    return function() {
        let count = 0;
        count++;
        return count;
    };
}

Debug the following C++ code, which leaks memory whenever an exception is thrown before its delete statement is reached.

int* data = new int[100];
processData(data);
delete[] data;

Debug the following Java code, which throws an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException intermittently depending on the input array's size.

for (int i = 0; i <= arr.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(arr[i]);
}

The following C code is meant to swap two integers using pointers, but the values remain unchanged after the function call. Identify the bug.

void swap(int a, int b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

The following C++ code produces an incorrect result because it performs integer division where floating-point division was intended. Identify and fix the bug.

int total = 7, count = 2;
double average = total / count;

Debug the following Python code, which is meant to remove items from a list while iterating over it but ends up skipping some elements.

for item in my_list:
    if item < 0:
        my_list.remove(item)

Debug the following C code, where data is written into a fixed-size buffer beyond its allocated bounds, leading to unpredictable behavior.

char buffer[5];
strcpy(buffer, "HelloWorld");

What is the output of the following Java code involving mixed logical and bitwise operators?

boolean result = (5 > 3) & (2 > 8);
System.out.println(result);

Predict the output of the following JavaScript code involving the comma operator used inside a for loop's update expression.

for (let i = 0, j = 10; i < 3; i++, j--) {
    console.log(i, j);
}

What is the output of the following JavaScript code involving a sparse array created using the delete operator?

let arr = [1, 2, 3];
delete arr[1];
console.log(arr.length, arr);

Predict the output of the following Java code involving integer division whose result is then assigned to a double variable.

double result = 7 / 2;
System.out.println(result);

What is the output of the following C++ code, and why is the order in which its function arguments are evaluated considered unspecified?

int x = 0;
int inc() { return ++x; }
cout << inc() << inc();

Predict the output of the following C++ code involving the increment operator applied directly to a pointer used in an array expression.

int arr[] = {1, 2, 3};
int *p = arr;
cout << *p++ << *p;

Predict the output of the following C code involving a macro that lacks proper parentheses around its parameter, causing unexpected expansion during arithmetic.

#define DOUBLE(x) x + x
printf("%d", 3 * DOUBLE(5));

Predict the output of the following code, which clears the rightmost set bit of a number using a bitwise AND trick.

int n = 12;
int result = n & (n - 1);

Predict the output of the following code, which isolates the rightmost set bit of a number using a two's-complement property.

int n = 10;
int result = n & (-n);

Predict the output of the following code, which checks whether the nth bit of an integer is set using a bitmask.

int n = 5;
int pos = 1;
int result = (n >> pos) & 1;
